questions.detailed:
Keyboard operability (WCAG 2.1.1) underpins most assistive technology: screen readers, switch devices, voice control and sip-and-puff systems all emulate keyboard input in some way. If something does not work from the keyboard, it commonly does not work for them either.

## Expected interactions

| Key | Typical behavior |
|---|---|
| Tab / Shift+Tab | Move to next or previous focusable element |
| Enter | Activate links and buttons |
| Space | Activate buttons, toggle checkboxes |
| Arrow keys | Move within tabs, menus, radio groups, listboxes (use roving focus or `aria-activedescendant`) |
| Escape | Close dialogs, menus and popups |
| Home / End | Jump to first or last item in some widgets |

## Principles

- **Logical focus order** (2.4.3): follows DOM order, so avoid positive `tabindex` values and CSS reordering that disconnects visual and DOM order.
- **No keyboard trap** (2.1.2): focus must be able to leave every component.
- **Skip link**: a first-focusable link such as "Skip to main content" bypasses repeated navigation (2.4.1).
- **Single-key shortcuts** (2.1.4): must be turnable off or remappable, since they conflict with speech input.
- Do not remove the focus outline without a replacement.

## Roving tabindex in React

```tsx
function Tabs({ items }: { items: string[] }) {
  const [active, setActive] = React.useState(0);
  const refs = React.useRef<(HTMLButtonElement | null)[]>([]);

  const onKeyDown = (e: React.KeyboardEvent) => {
    let next = active;
    if (e.key === "ArrowRight") next = (active + 1) % items.length;
    else if (e.key === "ArrowLeft") next = (active - 1 + items.length) % items.length;
    else return;
    e.preventDefault();
    setActive(next);
    refs.current[next]?.focus();
  };

  return (
    <div role="tablist" onKeyDown={onKeyDown}>
      {items.map((label, i) => (
        <button
          key={label}
          ref={(el) => { refs.current[i] = el; }}
          role="tab"
          aria-selected={i === active}
          tabIndex={i === active ? 0 : -1}
        >
          {label}
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

A real tabs implementation also needs `aria-controls` and `tabpanel` elements. Follow the ARIA Authoring Practices Guide.

## Common mistakes

Click handlers on non-interactive elements, hover-only menus, off-screen content that remains focusable, and custom components that capture Tab.

## Focus visible (2.4.7, AA)

Any keyboard-operable control must show a visible focus indicator. Browsers provide a default outline, and the most common failure is `outline: none` with nothing in its place. The modern approach is `:focus-visible`, which shows the ring for keyboard focus and generally not for mouse clicks on buttons.

```css
:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid #1a1a1a;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
/* Optional second ring so it works on light and dark backgrounds */
:focus-visible {
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 5px #ffffff;
}
```

The indicator should also satisfy the 3:1 non-text contrast guidance against adjacent colors. Test in forced-colors mode, where outlines are preserved but box-shadow is not.

## Focus not obscured, minimum (2.4.11, AA, new in 2.2)

When a component receives keyboard focus, it must not be **entirely** hidden by author-created content. Partially covered is allowed at AA (the AAA version, 2.4.12, requires no part to be hidden). Typical offenders are:

- sticky or fixed headers and footers,
- cookie or consent banners,
- chat launchers and floating action buttons,
- non-modal popups.

## Fixes

```css
html {
  scroll-padding-top: 5rem;   /* height of the sticky header */
  scroll-padding-bottom: 4rem;
}
```

`scroll-padding` makes the browser scroll focused elements into the visible region of the scroll container, not behind the fixed bar. Consent banners can be made dismissible or non-overlapping, and floating widgets can avoid covering the main content.

## Testing

Tab through long pages with sticky elements present and at narrow widths and 200% zoom, since overlays take proportionally more space on small viewports.

## Related note

Focus Appearance (2.4.13) in WCAG 2.2 is AAA, with size and contrast requirements for the indicator.

Relevant criteria include Error Identification (3.3.1), Labels or Instructions (3.3.2), Error Suggestion (3.3.3), Error Prevention for legal or financial submissions (3.3.4) and Status Messages (4.1.3).

## Pattern

1. Validate on submit (and optionally on blur after the first attempt).
2. Render a **summary** with links to each invalid field and move focus to it.
3. Render an inline message adjacent to each field, linked with `aria-describedby`.
4. Mark invalid fields with `aria-invalid="true"`.
5. Keep what the user typed.

## React example

```tsx
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

type Errors = { email?: string };

export function EnquiryForm() {
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState<Errors>({});
  const summaryRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  function onSubmit(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = String(new FormData(e.currentTarget).get("email") ?? "");
    const next: Errors = {};
    if (!/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+$/.test(email)) {
      next.email = "Enter an email address, for example name@example.com.";
    }
    setErrors(next);
    if (Object.keys(next).length) {
      // wait for render, then move focus to the summary
      requestAnimationFrame(() => summaryRef.current?.focus());
    }
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={onSubmit} noValidate>
      {errors.email && (
        <div ref={summaryRef} tabIndex={-1} role="alert" className="error-summary">
          <h2>There is a problem</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#email">{errors.email}</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
      <input
        id="email"
        name="email"
        type="email"
        autoComplete="email"
        aria-invalid={errors.email ? "true" : undefined}
        aria-describedby={errors.email ? "email-error" : undefined}
      />
      {errors.email && (
        <p id="email-error"><strong>Error:</strong> {errors.email}</p>
      )}
      <button type="submit">Send enquiry</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

The `Error:` prefix and message text mean that the error does not rely on color. A red border may be added as an extra cue.

## Notes and trade-offs

- `noValidate` disables native bubbles so that you control the messages. Native constraint validation is acceptable if its messages are clear and tested.
- Use either `role="alert"` or moving focus for the summary, not usually both with excessive noise. Test the announcement in a screen reader.
- Inline validation on every keystroke can be disruptive. Prefer validating after blur or submit.
- Include the format and an example, and for destructive or financial actions provide review, confirmation or undo.

## Common mistakes

Red border only, tooltip-only errors, errors that vanish on a timer, a focus reset to the top of the page, and clearing the form on failure.
